C.push(h2('1.3 Class Imbalance Mitigation Strategy'));
const prevA = FA.lmd ? pct(FA.lmd.attack_rate) : '8.06%'; const prevB = FA.mordor ? pct(FA.mordor.attack_rate) : '3.77%';
C.push(p([t('Selected technique: ', {bold: true}), t(`cost-sensitive learning with fold-wise class weights, plus threshold moving on a grouped hold-out inside the training fold — no resampling. Attack prevalence is ${prevA} in LMD-2023 and ${prevB} in Mordor. Random Forest uses class_weight = balanced_subsample (every bootstrap re-weighted to equal class mass), LightGBM scale_pos_weight = n_neg/n_pos of the training fold, the LSTM a class-weighted binary cross-entropy (pos_weight = n_neg/n_pos); the Isolation Forest is unsupervised and needs no balancing. The decision threshold of every model is the F1-maximising score on the inner hold-out of its fold.`)]));
C.push(p([t('Theoretical defence. ', {bold: true}), t(`Weighting minimises the importance-weighted empirical risk Σᵢ w(yᵢ)·ℓ(yᵢ, f(xᵢ)) with w(1) = n₀/n₁, which gives the two classes equal total weight in the training objective, without changing the data the model sees, leaving the FPR/FNR trade-off to the threshold. Random under-sampling to parity would discard ${FA.lmd ? pct(discardShare(FA.lmd.attack_rate), 0) : '—'}–${FA.mordor ? pct(discardShare(FA.mordor.attack_rate), 0) : '—'} of the benign rows, the only sample of each environment’s background and exactly what a detector must learn to reject. SMOTE-type interpolation is inappropriate here: the inputs are trailing counts, rates, binary flags and a nominal event category, so a convex combination of two attack rows is not an event that can occur, and the attack rows are dense bursts whose information is the window statistics, which interpolation smears (Milestone 2). No resampling variant or unweighted model was run, so no empirical claim is made for weighting over resampling: the choice is a priori; §2.2 measures the other half of the strategy, the threshold.`)]));
C.push(p([t('Leakage prevention. ', {bold: true}), t(`Folds are unions of whole groups g = (host, ⌊t/Δ⌋), Δ = ${(CFG.GROUP_MINUTES || {}).lmd || 10} min (LMD) and ${(CFG.GROUP_MINUTES || {}).mordor || 2} min (Mordor), so g(i) ≠ g(j) for every training row i and test row j; the inner hold-out V is a union of whole groups of the training fold with V ∩ test = ∅. Scaler medians and IQRs, class weights, early-stopping rounds, thresholds τ = argmax F1 over V (searched on a 200-quantile grid of the hold-out scores) and the cascade bands (400-quantile grid) are functions of the training fold only. What the grouping cannot remove is the trailing-window dependence at block boundaries (the one-hour out-degree window and the LSTM’s 20-event windows straddle blocks), so the split is a strict grouped split, not a temporal separation or a host hold-out${DIAG.lmd && DIAG.lmd.hosts_per_fold ? ` (every LMD fold contains ${Object.values(DIAG.lmd.hosts_per_fold).every(v => v === DIAG.lmd.n_hosts) ? 'all ' + DIAG.lmd.n_hosts : Math.min(...Object.values(DIAG.lmd.hosts_per_fold)) + '–' + Math.max(...Object.values(DIAG.lmd.hosts_per_fold)) + ' of the ' + DIAG.lmd.n_hosts} hosts, every Mordor fold ${DIAG.mordor && DIAG.mordor.hosts_per_fold ? (Object.values(DIAG.mordor.hosts_per_fold).every(v => v === DIAG.mordor.n_hosts) ? 'all ' + DIAG.mordor.n_hosts : Math.min(...Object.values(DIAG.mordor.hosts_per_fold)) + '–' + Math.max(...Object.values(DIAG.mordor.hosts_per_fold)) + ' of the ' + DIAG.mordor.n_hosts) : '—'})` : ''}; Milestone 2 showed that no chronological split of LMD-2023 gives a test period with both classes in adequate share.`)]));

C.push(h2('1.4 Validation Strategy & Explicit Hyperparameter Optimization'));
C.push(tref(`Validation: stratified group ${CFG.N_FOLDS || 5}-fold cross-validation (StratifiedGroupKFold, groups = host × time block) on every row, with a grouped hold-out inside each training fold — 20% of its groups (GroupShuffleSplit): ${rangePct(valShare('lmd', 'rf'))} of the rows on LMD but ${rangePct(valShare('mordor', 'rf'))} on Mordor, whose blocks differ greatly in size — for early stopping (LightGBM, LSTM) and the F1-optimal threshold; every metric is out-of-fold. Stratification is over groups, so positives balance only as far as the blocks allow: LMD folds hold ${posByFold('lmd', 'rf').length ? num(Math.min(...posByFold('lmd', 'rf'))) + '–' + num(Math.max(...posByFold('lmd', 'rf'))) : '—'} attack rows each, whereas Mordor’s attack rows cluster in a few dense blocks and one fold receives ${mPosMax ? num(mPosMax) + ' of the ' + num(FA.mordor.attack_rows) + ' (' + pct(mPosShare, 0) + ')' : '—'}, the other four ${mPos.length ? num(Math.min(...mPos)) + '–' + num(mPos.sort((a, b) => a - b)[3]) : '—'} — the asymmetry behind the fold variance of §1.5. Table @@hp@@ lists the hyper-parameters; all were fixed before the run and none was changed after seeing a test fold. One correction was made between the first run and this report, found in code review: LightGBM’s early stopping had also been tracking the validation log-loss (LightGBM’s default metric next to the requested AUC; the callback stops on whichever stagnates first, and on a class-weighted objective the log-loss can stagnate within a few rounds). The criterion was set to the pre-registered inner-validation AUC only and every LightGBM-dependent stage (cross-validation and reduced-feature runs, sensitivity sweep, transfer, cascade and LLM stage — the last with its prompt corrected at the same time: the placeholder masking and the score-with-threshold wording that §4.2 now shows; the interrupted first LLM run was never reported) was recomputed; the superseded LightGBM cross-validation files are kept with the results.${(() => { const l = lgbmChange('lmd'), m = lgbmChange('mordor'), r = lgbmChange('mordor', true); if (!l || !m) return ''; return ` Effect: the stopping round changed in ${l.nChanged + m.nChanged} of the 10 folds${r ? ' and ' + r.nChanged + ' of the 5 reduced-set folds' : ''} (per-fold values in the code README); out-of-fold F1 moved from ${f4(l.f1Before)} to ${f4(l.f1After)} on LMD and from ${f4(m.f1Before)} to ${f4(m.f1After)} on Mordor${r ? ' (reduced set ' + f4(r.f1Before) + ' → ' + f4(r.f1After) + ')' : ''}, so the first run’s conclusions stand; Mordor fold 0’s two-round stop is the inner AUC’s own optimum (§1.5).`; })()}`));
const hp = [['Random Forest', 'n_estimators = 300; max_depth = 12; min_samples_leaf = 20; max_features = √d (set explicitly); class_weight = balanced_subsample', 'more trees lower the vote variance; depth 12 and 20 rows per leaf limit memorisation of rare values or single hosts (Milestone-2 finding); √d de-correlates trees; balanced_subsample is the cost-sensitive loss'],
  ['LightGBM', 'n_estimators ≤ 600 with early stopping (50 rounds without improvement of the inner-validation AUC, the only metric tracked); learning_rate = 0.05; num_leaves = 31 (set explicitly); min_child_samples = 100; subsample = 0.8 (freq 1); colsample_bytree = 0.8; reg_lambda = 1; scale_pos_weight = n_neg/n_pos', `early stopping sets the rounds from the inner hold-out (used: ${lgbmIter('lmd').join(', ')} on LMD, ${lgbmIter('mordor').join(', ')} on Mordor); 31 leaves bound tree complexity; 100 rows per leaf and L2 suppress splits on rare values; subsampling adds diversity; the positive weight is the imbalance remedy`],
  ['Isolation Forest', 'n_estimators = 200; max_samples = 4096; contamination = auto (set explicitly, unused: the score is the anomaly percentile among the training rows and the threshold comes from the inner hold-out)', 'more trees stabilise path lengths; 4096-row subsamples keep isolation depth meaningful on millions of rows; the percentile mapping makes scores comparable across folds and datasets; the fit is label-free, only the threshold uses labels'],
  ['LSTM', `T = ${(CFG.LSTM_PARAMS || {}).T || 20} events per host window ending at every ${(CFG.LSTM_STRIDE || {}).lmd || 4}th (LMD) / ${(CFG.LSTM_STRIDE || {}).mordor || 2}nd (Mordor) event; stacked LSTM 64 → 32 units; dropout 0.3; batch-norm; Adam lr = 10⁻³; batch 1024; ≤ 8 epochs, patience 2 on inner AUC; pos_weight = n_neg/n_pos; inputs robust-scaled with the event category one-hot over the ${cv('lmd', 'lstm') ? cv('lmd', 'lstm').n_features_input - 34 : 26} registry levels (D = ${cv('lmd', 'lstm') ? cv('lmd', 'lstm').n_features_input : 60}); zero pre-padding, unmasked`, 'the window length sets the host history seen; two layers give a compact hierarchical state; dropout and early stopping control over-fitting on correlated windows; the weighted loss is the imbalance remedy; the window is labelled by its last event, so the decision is causal and per event']];
