C.push(tref(`On LMD-2023 the supervised models reach F1 ${cvm('lmd', 'rf') ? f3(Math.min(cvm('lmd', 'rf').f1, cvm('lmd', 'lgbm').f1, cvm('lmd', 'lstm').f1)) : '—'}–${cvm('lmd', 'rf') ? f3(Math.max(cvm('lmd', 'rf').f1, cvm('lmd', 'lgbm').f1, cvm('lmd', 'lstm').f1)) : '—'} against the 0.994 of [2], the LSTM among them ${cvm('lmd', 'lstm') ? f3(cvm('lmd', 'lstm').f1) : '—'} against 0.956 for the per-event LSTM of [2] — comparable to the published shallow models and above the published LSTM, although this pipeline excludes the papers’ seven raw calendar fields and the capture identifiers (CompSTA, EventRecordID) that [2, 3] use and Milestone 2 showed to be proxies of the capture schedule, and evaluates under grouped cross-validation with inner-validation thresholds instead of random splits. That the numbers stay this high without those columns shows the LMD-2023 capture to be separable by structure (Milestone 2 §3.3–4.1), not detection power, as the transfer results confirm. The unsupervised comparison agrees: [4] reports ≈95% AUC for unsupervised detectors on LMD-2023 and our Isolation Forest reaches ${cvm('lmd', 'if') ? f3(cvm('lmd', 'if').roc_auc) : '—'} AUC but only F1 ${cvm('lmd', 'if') ? f3(cvm('lmd', 'if').f1) : '—'}, because an anomaly ranking must still be thresholded and LMD’s non-network benign events are as anomalous to it as its attacks; [4] also reports ≈93% F1, so the ranking is comparable and the operating point is not. No published number exists for Mordor APT29 under provenance labels; the closest analogue, the label-free Isolation Forest of [11] (AUC 0.825 on insider-threat data), compares with our ${cvm('mordor', 'if') ? f3(cvm('mordor', 'if').roc_auc) : '—'} AUC and far lower F1 (${cvm('mordor', 'if') ? f3(cvm('mordor', 'if').f1) : '—'}) on Mordor, for the same thresholding reason. The session-level LANL results [1, 5] classify reconstructed sessions or user-behaviour windows, not events, and are not comparable. Unlike the cited studies, this report gives FPR and FNR together with a cross-environment test (Figure ${FIGN['cross_dataset.png']}); [5] reports recall and FPR but no precision or F1, and [1]’s generalisation figure (average precision 0.95 on unseen sources) is within one environment.`));

